Q2. What Challenges Did You Face in Project?

Challenge 1 — Performance Issue in Large File Processing
Reconciliation process was taking too much time for large transaction files.
Root cause: Nested loops caused high time complexity.
old logic: 
for(i=0; i<n; i++)
{
    for(j=0; j<n; j++)
    {
        // matching logic
    }
}

sloution:
You can store both files in map and compare using keys.
This completely avoids nested loops and makes reconciliation cleaner.

#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <string>

using namespace std;

int main()
{
    // File A Transactions
    map<string, int> fileA;

    fileA["TXN1001"] = 500;
    fileA["TXN1002"] = 700;
    fileA["TXN1003"] = 900;

    // File B Transactions
    map<string, int> fileB;

    fileB["TXN1002"] = 700;
    fileB["TXN1003"] = 900;
    fileB["TXN1004"] = 1000;

    // Reconciliation Logic
    map<string, int>::iterator it;

    for (it = fileA.begin(); it != fileA.end(); ++it)
    {
        // Search same transaction in File B
        if (fileB.find(it->first) != fileB.end())
        {
            cout << it->first << " Matched" << endl;
        }
        else
        {
            cout << it->first << " Missing in File B" << endl;
        }
    }

    return 0;
}

New logic using map gives:

Direct lookup by key
Faster searching
Cleaner code
Better scalability

“In reconciliation, both transaction files were large, so nested loop comparison caused performance issues.
I redesigned the solution using STL maps for both files, where transaction ID was used as the key.
This allowed direct key-based lookup instead of repeated iteration, reducing complexity and improving reconciliation speed significantly.”

“Why map and not vector?”
-> vector requires linear search
map provides fast lookup using tree structure
Better for reconciliation/search-heavy systems


You can additionally say:

“If ordering is not required, unordered_map can further improve average lookup to O(1).”


