1. What is a Smart Pointer?

A smart pointer is a C++ object that manages the lifetime of dynamically allocated memory automatically.

It helps avoid:

Memory leaks
Dangling pointers
Manual delete
Double free issues

What is unique_ptr?

unique_ptr provides:

Single ownership
Only one pointer can own the object

Cannot copy.
e.g. #include <iostream>
#include <memory>

using namespace std;

int main()
{
    unique_ptr<int> p1(new int(100));

    cout << *p1 << endl;

    return 0;
}


Q. Why can't unique_ptr be copied?

Because only one owner is allowed.

Q. How to transfer ownership in unique_ptr?

Using move().


**. What is shared_ptr?

shared_ptr allows:

Multiple pointers to share ownership

Uses:

Reference counting

Memory is deleted only when count becomes zero.


#include <iostream>
#include <memory>

using namespace std;

int main()
{
    shared_ptr<int> p1(new int(100));

    shared_ptr<int> p2 = p1;

    cout << p1.use_count() << endl;

    return 0;
}

What is weak_ptr?

weak_ptr is used with shared_ptr.

It:

Does NOT increase reference count
Prevents circular dependency

Problem:

A points to B
B points to A

Reference count never becomes 0.

Result:

Memory leak

